    "title": "Matthew Kuhnemann's Bowling Action Under Review",
    "text": "• Several senior players of Sri Lanka expressed concerns about Matthew Kuhnemann's bowling action after the first test in Galle.\n• Concerns were raised regarding the legality of Kuhnemann's action, particularly after his performance where he took nine wickets.\n• The Cricket Players' Association (CPI) has officially reported Kuhnemann's bowling action for review.\n• Kuhnemann, aged 28, was the leading wicket-taker in the series with 16 wickets at an average of 17.18.\n• He must undergo independent tests within the next two weeks to determine if he can continue bowling internationally.\n• The ICC reported Kuhnemann's action after the second test in Galle.\n• Some Sri Lanka batters had concerns about Kuhnemann's action during the first test.\n• Sri Lanka's rotating bowling coach, Piyal Wijetunge, reviewed the action but found nothing unusual.\n• Despite the coach's assessment, some players remained concerned about Kuhnemann's deliveries.\n• Kuhnemann has returned to Australia after the series and is expected to play for Tasmania in domestic cricket.\n• He did not play in Tasmania's match against Victoria after returning to Brisbane for a medical check-up.\n• Players with reported suspicious bowling actions can continue playing domestic cricket until tested but cannot play internationally.\n• A date for Kuhnemann's biomechanical test has not been established but must occur within two weeks of being reported.\n• The test will take place at the National Cricket Center in Brisbane under the supervision of CPI specialists.\n• Kuhnemann will need to bowl at a similar speed and with similar revolutions as in the second test during the evaluation.\n• If his elbow extension exceeds the allowed 15 degrees, he may be prohibited from bowling until he corrects his action.",
